INT: How aware was your family-- how much did they realize what was going on in Germany just across the border?

GD: Oh, we knew. We knew. Oh, we didn't in Germany, you know. We didn't know. We knew after the bombing when they took France, half zone, first, they took only half of France. And we were not too far from the demarcation line. And we heard that, yeah, this first they ask us to register as a Jew to the mayor office. So my father did it. And then they started a sanction. You cannot go out to buy food after 3 o'clock. You cannot be out in the street at 8 o'clock. You cannot go to a museum. You cannot go to park. You can't-- the only movie we saw, it was-- it was against the Jew. It was film, it was showing that a Jew is taking the world. And it was horrible. I went because we were curious. My sister took me there, she-- my older sister. And my mother was not happy that we went there. But anyway, there was a lot of, you know, advertising. They put on my father's store they put enterprise, Jew's enterprise. And the Germans used to come and take whatever they want, and they didn't pay for it. You know, we're starting to get bad, you know? And then we put the star. When they start to put the Jewish star, I was six years old. And I wore a David star on my clothes. And my mother, since she had seven children, so it was allowed to do. But-- and they said that we were going to go to Germany. First he took all the [? central ?] Jew, all the foreigners. So we were French. So we didn't think they were going to-- Marshal PÃ©tain, you know the Marshal PÃ©tain said he was going to protect the French Jews. But he didn't. He had to give it up. You know, he was forced. And then they started to tell us that we're going to go to Germany for three months, or a few months, and we're going back home. You know? We have to help Germany to produce the ammunition, food, or whatever. It looked normal. It doesn't look too-- a lot of people went. They didn't ask question and they went. All my street, a lot of them, you know? And then my father knew somebody at the mayor's office. He was liked. So he told him, he says somebody warned me. He says, you better leave town. The thing is going to get bad. And we had only a couple of weeks to get ready. We find a man. It was very expensive to cross the borders, you know? You had the German like that. But a few hundred feet, you know? And they pass by. They go by like that. And you have to cross when they are at the extremities. If they hear you, they shoot you. They were shooting everybody who was passing. And another Jew got killed like that. But we got very lucky. This guy didn't want to-- this guy gave us two-- he says, we cannot do two group. I cannot take everybody. So we were four children who couldn't go with the-- so my mother had an idea. She went to see the director of the school, you know? The principal the school, I don't know what you call it. And the principal of the school was liked-- I have a brother who had a-- he had a heart disease. And he got very friendly. My brother was a very good student. And he-- my mother begged him to give us a pass, to say we were going on vacation to the south. And she cried. She begged him. And he said, you know, Madame, he said, I'm taking a chance on my life, he said. But I'm going to give you that because I like your son and so on. So we were three children. We were just children. We didn't go we didn't go with the passeur, we called the passeur, the man who crossed the line with us. So we went alone. My brother was 13 and 1/2 years old. My sister was 12. I was about nine, I think, and my young brother, four. We took a train alone. Somebody put us in a train. And with a pass, we went to the demarcation line. And the Germans made us get out get out of the train. And you know what they're doing sometimes. They check the men pants to see if they have circumcision. And, but we are lucky, it didn't happen with our two brothers. But they even carry our bag, and we were so scared, you know, because my mother said to us, don't say anything. Don't. My mother put money in the lining of our coats, you know? And then I was very scared, you know? I was worried about the sugar more than I was about the money. But anyway, so we passed the line with the Germans. They helped us, put us in another train. And then we went to the town where we supposed to go. And then somebody, then we waited at the-- we were supposed-- a truck was supposed to pick us up, a company named [? Jericho. ?] And then we waited. We waited, waited. We arrived in the afternoon. It was getting late. It got 7 o'clock. The owner of the cafe, whatever, she came to us. She said, what are you doing here? She said, well, we're waiting for a truck. But he didn't show up. She said, I have to call the police. I say, no, no, please. We want to go on vacation. And we start to cry and all that. So she said, well, I have to. The police came. And he asked us where we were going. And we told him we're going to [? Aujan. ?] [? Aujan ?] is another town. It was 80 mile about from where we were. And I said he supposed to pick us up. He didn't come and something happened. He said, well, he said, I have to give you back, send you back home. And we said, no. Oh, no. We begged. We begged. We said, give us until tomorrow. We're sure he's going to come. And we are lucky with the owner of the cafe. She-- they called the refugee center. They picked us up for the night. And my brother had a terrible night because he was sick. He had-- his heart was pounding a lot. But we passed the morning. And the woman from the cafe, she invited us at her house. She gave us a nice lunch. And she said, I left a note at the cafe, that to pick you up. Don't worry. If he comes, he'll come here. And on the afternoon, he came. The truck came and he had a problem with the car or whatever. He couldn't make it. And he picked us up. And we met 80 miles away where we met my family, with two other groups who came with my mother, and my-- and my father left alone. I forgot to tell you. My father left alone with a little girl. And he got arrested too with the police, French. And he said, I have to give you to the Germans. But then he saw a little girl. My sister was five year old. And he said, I'll let you go. And he let him go. We got very lucky. But that was-- once we got to the free zone, we were on the free zone. Then we stay in hotel for a month and a half. It was very expensive, not a fancy hotel. But we stayed a long time. And then my father met somebody. He said, I have an old place if you want, I'll rent it to you very cheap. You can stay there. And we went there. It was a place. It was horrible. But you know, there was no floor. There was a coal storage, like a coal. So my mother cleaned the best as we can. There was a chimney where we put coal, put fire, or whatever. There was one floor. And it was pretty bad. But she said anything is better than Germany. You know? So we live like that. My mother would send us to school there, to a Catholic school with a priest. They knew we were Jewish. And they tried to convert us. We told them later. Later, we were talking to our parents. We're waiting. And then we-- something happened. My parents decided to go to Morocco or Algeria, wherever we could go. And they had tickets because we met a rabbi there. Oh, I didn't tell you. My brother died. One night, the day we were supposed to leave, my brother died. He got his clothes ready, on-- you know, on the bed. He was so excited. He got sick. And my mother 2:00 in the morning run in the street with my sister trying to find a doctor. And very few people answer. You know, somebody knock on your door in the morning. But somebody did finally. We got a doctor. It was 4 o'clock in the morning. And he tried-- he looked at him. He said, he's very bad. He put some leech. He had a blood coagulating around his heart. He should have taken him to the hospital, but I guess he couldn't. But it didn't work. So by about 5:00 in the morning, he died. He died in my father's arms, yeah. And then my mother went crazy, and the rabbi came. We did the funeral. And we had, you know, we have to wait eight days. Because it's the Shiva. You know, we sit Shiva. And we went to Marseille to the port, where we had a boat waiting for us. And when we got to Marseille, everything are closed. The Americans got in Marseille, in Africa, North Africa. I had two uncles, one in the American army, the [NON-ENGLISH] from Algeria. And all the ports were closed. So we got stuck. We got stuck in Marseille. And we stayed in a hotel again at the port. A lot of Jewish people were there from all the countries-- Hungarian all kinds of nationalities. We met a lot of people. But then it got so expensive, we couldn't do it. We send all our money to Morocco. Our money was in Morocco through a friend. We send money there. So we got stuck with very little money. So my mother went to a refugee center. She said, look. We got stuck here, you know. And because my father was born in Morocco, so they gave us-- they gave him the-- they gave him the-- they gave us a place to stay. I mean it was a big refugee center. It was cold. It was the food was terrible. The window was open. You force it. It was they had the strong wind in the winter, a [? missile, ?] they call it. It was very, very difficult. We got sick. We had no food, very little food. They used to give you a piece of onion, with the water that was the soup, you know? It was very-- you know. And we start to get disease on the skin. So my mother put us in a hospital there in France. And you see-- and it was Christmas time and we had a great meal. And we told my mother, don't come back for a few weeks. We're going to eat some. We're going to-- they have food here. And she did. And from there we're going to send-- we were sent to another center, in a village in Provence, in Provence up in the mountain. OK.

