GD: Oh, yeah we were. We were always concerned. But they didn't, because my mother was French, they never bothered us for a long time, until '42, '42, July '42, we left our home, and we came back in '45, I think, '45 yeah. And when we came back, when we were bombed by the American, we were listening to the BBC, you know the-- they used to say to us-- we're going to bomb pretty bad your town. You better go in the mountain. Go tonight. You know? You've got to leave your homes. We did. We went with buckets of water, or a shovel. And we went in the mountain like a group, you know, looking for a place to spend the night. And-- but it was-- we were bombed by-- I saw-- like what you call those, when somebody shoots, you know, we used to see metal, metal over fire, around us. It was terrible. We used to lie down. Soon as a bomb would come down, we would lie down. But finally we-- in the morning, it stopped. And we came back home. And the Germans, you know, the Germans left. They left. They killed a lot of-- there was a lot of French underground, you know? They were helping, you know, the American. My father was there found a job like a few days a week, he would go to guard in a mountain, like for electric plant. Electric plant, he was guarding. And the American came. They parachute there. My father helped them, and he let him sleep in this cave with him. And he had all his Hebrew books. He was praying. We were afraid. Of course, we were afraid they could kill. The German could have picked us up any time. Because the Germans came in free zone, you know? When we were in the mountain. But anyway, he saw a lot of parachutists, American. He brought home those parachutes, the material. We made shirts with it, you know? It was amazing. They used to give us chocolate, gum, stockings. And we used to give them. We used to give them a vegetable. They were dying for tomato, for fruit, for that kind of thing. And one time, they camped all around our-- there was a square around where the house where we were, a little house. And they came there with tents. And that's when we, you know, and I met a lot of Americans. And I hurt myself in a fountain. And I cut my hand was cut badly. And a doctor, he took his helmet, and he cleaned my hand. He put a bandage. And you know, it was interesting. I was talking with the-- I didn't speak English then. But I was talking with the dictionary. With them, you know he was-- And then they came to town, you there was, you know, they had tanks. They have auto amphibian, the water-- the auto the car that goes in the water, you know? They took us for a ride. We had a lot of Jewish guys we brought home. In my home, we brought all kinds of people. There were the Chinese people who went by. And you know, all, and then Africans, and then you know we give them, you know, food, you know I mean fresh food. You know what I mean? Yeah. So we were. But they all went to Italy. And a lot of them died, Americans who went to Italy. That was bad in Italy. A lot of them died, I'm sure. I heard about it. Yeah. But that was it. And we came back after the war.

INT: During your time in the free zone and when you were avoiding being caught, how did your family sustain itself? What were your parents able to do?

GD: Sustain? You mean how do we eat?

