INT: Where did your family get the money to buy food or to pay for shelter?

GD: Well my father had money. We had a store, you know. We had some savings. So you know, so we live with that. But when you know, and-- but we made a decision to leave France. So they send some money there. They send mattresses. They send clothes. You know, we send everything before. Our plan was to leave. The rabbi told us. There was a big rabbi, a very important rabbi in my town. And he told us he warned us to leave. But a lot of them got taken in my town, very few left, very few.

INT: That was my next question. During the war, how much did your family know about how Jews were being treated in Poland and Germany?

GD: We didn't know. We didn't know it was a camp dying, we had no way. We knew it wasn't-- my cousin went. My cousin went to-- he got taken with his father. And he wrote a letter from Drancy. All the Jews were taken to Drancy. We got a letter from Drancy, but after that nothing. So they must have gone to Auschwitz. I think he went to Auschwitz. You know, he died. He was playing-- he was a friend. He was a good friend of my brother. They played together. And it's funny. He died and my brother died too of sickness. You know? That's a lot of people died. Some women, some women, we have caves in France. I know women, they came to pick her up, you know? She had about four kids. And the husband was getting dressed and the boys too, the two boys. But she took the little one and she went out of the house, because there is caves, and you can go by the back of the house. They look for her the all night with the lights, you know? And she made it. She made it.

She died not long ago. But she's made it. A lot of people, you know, tried to go, to leave. We knew something was bad, but not that bad. No, it took a long time before we understood that. But you knew something was wrong because we never get letters from people anymore. So we knew something was wrong. You know?

