INT: When you look back on the war years, can you share with us any close calls any times you were almost caught?

GD: Oh, yeah. Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. Yeah, one time, you know, we had a second floor. This was at the beginning. There was a big, a captain, SS, I mean a German captain, you know? And he looked up. My sister spit on him from the window. And he looked up. And he didn't do anything. I was there and we thought he was going to kill all of us. But he didn't. He walked away. And also, we used to go to an American, what do you call, a help? It was an underground. We used to take a bus from where I was two hours, go to Marseille. And we used to take pots and pans, small, but we used to get food from the American Red Cross-- no, underground, underground. And you had to be very discreet, you know. It was a small door where you go in, you know. And one time we went. We used to bring soups of meat. And they give us a lot of stuff good. And then we took a bus to go there. And then we walked. We were about to go in. And my mother didn't put anybody who puts her eyes up to that door, they take him in. They take him in. You are arrested. My mother saw the boots. She kept walking. She didn't look up. She looked at the boot. And one time, we went for matzah. She broke the bag into-- from the German the broken matzah, one bag got a little open. You looked at it. I don't think he knew what it was. And we got-- in a bus, we used to have ID card. ID card, we put a stamp. It's customary in France to put a stamp, you know? And my father when they put stamp, Jew on the card. We put stamp. But if we took the stamp off, they couldn't see. On the bus, they would take your ID card, you know? And I know my mother feared. And she gave it to me. I was so scared to death. But they didn't. They ask a few people, but my mother they didn't ask. They would find it. We could have taken. Another night, we were in that little town in Provence. There came drunk Germans, about five of them. They came, and my mother said that's it. We better get dressed. You know, many times we had the fear. Let's get dressed. They're coming to pick us up. And they were downstairs. They were drunk. They were drinking and singing. And that was it. It just happened to be in front of our place. But you know, there was always fear, no matter where you went, you were with the German, you know. It was scary, very scary. I still dream about it. I still dream they try to shoot me. Isn't that something? At my age. You don't forget. It disturbs you. But you know, I don't do it all the time. But sometimes I do dream.

But many times I don't want to go into it. I mean my parents were the ones who worry, not us, but them. My mother with asthma, my father had a kidney disease. They were unwell. But they're great parents. They've been wonderful. They used to have a piece of bread. When we went to the refugee center, they used to get their bread and give it to us. They didn't eat any bread, my parents. You know? So it was a tough war.

