GD: Well, they put me in a plane or something. I forgot, train. A train? I don't remember too well. I was 12, 11 or 12, I think. Yeah. Yeah. But that was a good-- after the war, what a peace-- you know, it was peaceful, you know. Yeah. And--

INT: And how long? How long were you in Switzerland?

GD: Oh, about four months. I don't remember, four to six months. I don't remember too well actually. I don't remember. Four months it was sure, I know. They put me in school. It was very cold. I remember that, freezing. But I loved it. It was nice.

INT: Then you came back to your family?

GD: Back to my family, yes. My parents-- we didn't get a home right away. We got the store. We got robbed of everything, of course. The apartment too, we have not-- people moved in our apartment. They didn't pay rent. The Germans told them they didn't have to pay rent. Our house was occupied too with-- what do you call? A veteran, French veteran, so it was very hard to get him out. He had a leg, no leg, amputated, with the seven children. And it took us three months to get our home. So we stayed-- oh wait, after the war we stayed in a refugee center for three months, before we got our home. Yeah. But we did. And my father started again, slowly. It was difficult. But they did OK, fair, you know? They were getting old, you know?

INT: And what about you rebuilding your life after the war? What were some of the first things that you did?

